          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#local_definition_as_functors_on_artinian_objects Local definition as functors on Artinian objects
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+context"} deformation context
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi α
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{Y}, \{E_\alpha\}_\alpha)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
              of
              %strong formal moduli problems
              over it is the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} sub-(∞,1)-category
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaves"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-presheaves
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mi inf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}^{inf}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Moduli
                      %mi 𝒴
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mi inf
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Moduli^\mathcal{Y}
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    [\mathcal{Y}^{inf}, \infty Grpd]
            %p
              on those
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mi inf
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \colon \mathcal{Y}^{inf} \to \infty Grpd
              such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  over the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                  they are
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(*) \simeq *
                  (hence they are
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-reduced+object"} anti-reduced
              %li
                %p
                  they preserve
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-pullbacks"} (∞,1)-pullbacks
                  of small morphisms (are
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%E2%88%9E-presheaf+on+E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} infinitesimally cohesive
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, Def. 1.1.14 with Def. 1.1.8
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This means that a “formal deformation problem” is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
              whose geometric structure is detected by the “test spaces” in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}^{op}
              in a way that respects gluing (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"}> descent
              ) in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}^{op}
              as given by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-pullbacks"} (∞,1)-pullbacks
              there. The first condition requires that there is an essentially unique such probe by the point, hence that these higher geometric space has essentially a single global point. This is the condition that reflects the infinitesimal nature of the deformation problem.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The clause about pullbacks is what makes the behaviour at arbitrary infinitesimal order be all controlled by that at first order, see
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#CalaqueGrivaux18"} Calaque &amp; Grivaux 2018, top of p. 8
            %p
              This ability to understand deformations order-by-order is related to the existence of a good obstruction theory. Indeed, evaluating a formal moduli problem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              on a pullback diagram defining an elementary morphism exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo −
                        %mi n
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(\Omega^{\infty - n}E)
              as an obstruction space.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToLInfinityAlgebras
            Relation to
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
            0, write write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi CAlg
                    %mi k
                    %mi sm
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi CAlg
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CAlg_k^{sm} \hookrightarrow CAlg_k
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+ring"} Artinian
            connective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} E-∞ algebras
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebras"} dg-algebras
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            The smallness condition implies connectivity (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, prop. 1.1.11 (1)
            ), hence that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} E-∞ algebras
            vanish in negative degree. Notice that for the dg-algebras this means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
            vanishes in negative degree
            %em if
            the differential is taken to have degree -1 (see
            %a{:href => "#Porta13"} Porta 13, def. 3.1.14
            for emphasis). This is the natural condition for the function algebra in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            = succeed "/dg-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi k
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Moduli
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi CAlg
                          %mi k
                          %mi sm
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    L_\infty Alg_k \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} Moduli^{CAlg^{sm}_k}
            %p
              with that of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} L-∞ algebras
          %p
            In this form this is (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, theorem 0.0.13
            ), originally proved as (
            %a{:href => "#Pridham"}> Pridham, theorem 2.30 and proposition 4.42
            ). See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} model structure for L-∞ algebras
            for various other incarnations of this equivalence.
          %h3#relation_to_lie_differentiation Relation to Lie differentiation
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+context"} deformation context
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒴
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Yoneda+embedding"} (∞,1)-Yoneda embedding
              gives an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lie
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒴
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Moduli
                      %mi 𝒴
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Lie \colon \mathcal{Y} \to Moduli^{\mathcal{Y}}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒴
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \in \mathcal{Y}^{op}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lie
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lie(Y)
              represents the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal neighbourhood
              of the basepoint of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              as seen by the infinitesimally thickened points dual to the
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{E_\alpha\}
            %p
              Hence we may call this the operaton of
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+differentiation"} Lie differentiation
              of spaces in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒴
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}^{op}
              around their given base point.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+problem"} moduli problem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} model structure for L-∞ algebras
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+of+L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} sheaf of L-∞ algebras
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} synthetic differential ∞-groupoid
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Pridham
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Pridham"}> Jonathan Pridham
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Unifying derived deformation theories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0344"} arXiv:0705.0344
            %li#Lurie
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Formal+moduli+problems"} Formal moduli problems
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                %em Moduli problems for ring spectra
                ICM 2010 proceedings contribution
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/moduli.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Porta13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mauro+Porta"}> Mauro Porta
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Derived formal moduli problems
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://algant.eu/documents/theses/porta.pdf"} pdf
            %li#CalaqueGrivaux18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Damien+Calaque"}> Damien Calaque
                , Julien Grivaux:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal moduli problems and formal derived stacks
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Derived Algebraic Geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 55
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.09556"} arXiv:1802.09556
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://jgrivaux.perso.math.cnrs.fr/articles/fmp.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/view?ProductCode=PASY/55"} ISBN:978-2-85629-938-8
            %li
              %p
                Arjen Baarsma:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Deformation problems and
                    = succeed "-algebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi L
                              %mn ∞
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dspace.library.uu.nl/handle/1874/386311"} dspace:1874/386311
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://webspace.science.uu.nl/~caval101/homepage/Students_files/BaarsmaPhD.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Brice Le Grignou, Victor Roca i Lucio:
                %em A new approach to formal moduli problems
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07227"}> arXiv:2306.07227
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The correspondence between formal moduli problems and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-Lie+algebras"} dg-Lie algebras
            is extended to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+characteristic"} positive characteristic
            in
          %ul
            %li#BrantnerMathew19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lukas+Brantner"}> Lukas Brantner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Akhil+Mathew"} Akhil Mathew
              = succeed "," do
                %em Deformation Theory and Partition Lie Algebras
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.07352"} 1904.07352
